# Invoke task catalog

Every task callable via `./workflow.cmd <namespace>.<task>`. Tasks are defined in `_CI/tasks/<module>.py` and registered in `_CI/tasks/__init__.py`.

For why a knob is a sub-task, a flag or an environment variable — and what every variable this
workflow reads does — see [Environment variables and flags](environment-and-flags.md).

## Top-level shortcuts

The shortcuts below resolve to a namespace's default task (the one added with `default=True`).

| Task | Default of | What it does |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `bootstrap` | `bootstrap.all` | First-run setup; idempotent. Pass `--force` to re-run. |
| `preflight` | `preflight.all` | **Everything, then the derived files.** The one to run before opening a pull request. |
| `format` | `format.all` | Ruff format + import sort. `--paths="<paths>"` limits it to files you name; the commit hook prints that command scoped to whatever it just checked. |
| `lint` | `lint.all` | Runs ruff, the formatting check, validate-pyproject, pylint, ty, complexipy, commitizen. |
| `test` | `test.all` | pytest with coverage and xdist. |
| `build` | `build.all` | SBOM compose + validate, then `uv build` and the distribution checks (`twine check --strict`, `check-wheel-contents`). No dependency audit — see `secure` — and no README badge: the CI badge points at {% if git_hosting_service == 'github' %}GitHub's workflow-status endpoint{% else %}GitLab's pipeline-status endpoint{% endif %}, which is always current. |
| `release` | `release.all` | Validate, branch, bump, changelog, push, open PR/MR. |
| `quality` | `quality.all` | `quality.pyscn`: pyscn analysis (HTML and JSON, not opened) and the pass/fail check. Reports whether the badge matches; `--write` updates it. |
| `secure` | `secure.all` | Every security task at once: pip-audit + SBOM compose/validate. Nothing automated calls it; the pieces have their own homes. |
| `document` | `document.all` | properdocs build + view in browser. |
| `container` | `container.publish` | Build deps image; in CI pushes to the registry. |

## `preflight` — the one command that does it all

| Task | Args | What it does |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `preflight` | `--write`, `--audit-dependencies` | Verify formatting, lint, `pyproject.toml` against the packaging specs, type-check, the commit messages this push adds, the docs build, pyscn, the full tox matrix, the wheel and sdist, and the README badges plus the coverage ratchet against what the tools just measured — failing with everything out of date. This bare command is what the pre-push hook and the CI pipeline run. `--write` updates the badges and the ratchet instead of comparing them; it is opt-in because a command named for an inspection should not modify the tree. Never edits source either way — unformatted code fails and points at `format`. `--audit-dependencies` adds the pip-audit run, off by default because its answer depends on the advisory database rather than on your tree. |
| `preflight.staged` | `--paths="<paths>"` | Only the checks that can be judged from the given files alone, and by default only the files you have staged. What the pre-commit hook runs. Reports rather than fixes, like every other entry point — unformatted code names `format`. |

It checks four badges fully: coverage and pyscn from the reports the run just produced, and
version and Python range from `pyproject.toml`. The fifth, the CI badge, is *written* by
`--write` and only half checked. Its URL comes from `git remote get-url origin`, which is a fact
about your clone rather than about this tree, so comparing it would fail a gate run for a
contributor whose remote is a fork — their fix would point your badge at their fork. What the
gate does ask is narrower and answerable from the tree: whether the shipped placeholder is still
there once `origin` resolves, which means nobody has written it yet.
{% if git_hosting_service == 'github' %}GitHub{% else %}GitLab{% endif %} renders the status
itself once the URL is right, so run `preflight --write` after `git remote add origin …` and
commit the README.

Both read one registry — `STEPS` in `_CI/tasks/preflight.py` — which declares every check
once with its scope, its path filter and whether it writes. The two hooks and the CI job consume
that declaration rather than keeping their own lists, so a check added to the registry reaches
its tier without editing three files.

Both modes run the *same* steps; only the derived-files step differs, by swapping one
callable. Every derived value is written through a single function taking `write`,
so what verifying compares is exactly what writing would have produced — a separate
implementation for the verifying side is how a gate drifts from its generator.

Neither mode edits your code. Formatting is verified here and fixed by `./workflow.cmd format`,
named for the mutation it performs: a command you run before opening a pull request should not
reformat something you never looked at and fold it into your commit.

The bare `preflight` is what the pre-push hook and the entire CI pipeline run, so reproducing a
pipeline failure means typing the command you see in the log. It does refresh `reports/` —
pytest's coverage JSON and pyscn's analysis are the *inputs* it compares against, and both are
gitignored. What it never touches is a tracked file.

## `develop` — local dev environment

| Task | Args | What it does |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `bootstrap` | `--force` | Run all bootstrap steps. Idempotent. Its own namespace, so `./workflow.cmd bootstrap`. |
| `develop.pre-commit-install` | — | Install the hooks into `.git/hooks/` (all three stages at once). |
| `develop.pre-commit` | — | A dry run of your next commit: the commit-stage hooks over the files you staged, run through pre-commit itself so it exercises the hook wiring (`files:` filters, `SKIP`, the entry itself), not just the checks. Read-only, like the hooks it runs. Not the full check either; `preflight` is what runs everything, including every file rather than the staged ones. |
| `develop.pylock` | — | Export the locked environment, every group included, as a standard `reports/pylock.toml` (PEP 751) for tools that do not read `uv.lock`. See [Export a pylock file](../how-to/export-a-pylock-file.md). |
| `develop.bump-uv` | `--version=<v>` | Move every uv pin to the newest release at least 7 days old: the four version literals, the base image's tag *and* its re-resolved digest, and `uv.lock`. Warns when the bump crosses a minor version. `--version` takes a specific release, ignoring the cool-down. See [Dependency groups](dependency-groups.md#the-uv-pin-and-the-one-command-that-moves-it). |

## `format`, `lint`, `test`, `quality`

Every task in this group that takes paths accepts `--paths="<space-separated paths>"`, and
defaults to the whole project when it is omitted. `preflight.staged` uses it to check only the
staged files, applying each step's own filter — see
[`.pre-commit-config.yaml`](configuration-files.md#pre-commit-configyaml) for how the hook
feeds it, and the registry in `_CI/tasks/preflight.py` for which steps can be narrowed at all.

Calling one directly is how to run a single tool over a single file:
`./workflow.cmd lint.pylint --paths="src/thing.py"`.

| Task | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| `format.ruff` | `ruff check --select I --fix` + `ruff format`. |
| `lint.ruff` | `ruff check`. |
| `lint.format-check` | `ruff format --check`: reports unformatted files without rewriting them. What `preflight` runs; `format` is what fixes them. |
| `lint.pylint` | `pylint src/ _CI/tasks/ tests/`. |
| `lint.ty` | `ty check src/ _CI/tasks/ tests/`. |
| `lint.pyproject` | `validate-pyproject pyproject.toml`: the `[project]` table, `[dependency-groups]` and the `[tool.*]` tables it has schemas for, against the PyPA specifications. Runs on any commit that stages `pyproject.toml`. See [Packaging conformance](../explanation/packaging-conformance.md). |
| `lint.complexipy` | Cognitive complexity check on `src/`. |
| `lint.commitizen` | Validate commit messages since last tag. |
| `test.pytest` | pytest with coverage, xdist, HTML reports on the active interpreter. Forward extra pytest args with `--args="…"` (e.g. `--args="-k test_hello"`). Writes no badge: one interpreter is not what the coverage badge means. |
| `test.tox` | Full multi-version tox matrix in parallel, then `coverage combine` into one `reports/coverage.json` — the union across interpreters, which is what the badge and the ratchet read. The only test command whose number *is* the badge's, so it reports whether the badge still matches and updates it with `--write`. `--env=py313` runs a single environment, which suppresses both and refuses `--write`. |
| `test.coverage` | Print the coverage report from the latest run and render the browsable HTML into `reports/coverage/`. After `test.tox` that is the union across the matrix. This is where the HTML comes from: `preflight` produces only the JSON it reads. Writes no badge and says nothing about one — it reports whatever is in the data file, so it cannot know whether the badge is stale. |
| `test.view` | Open HTML test + coverage reports in browser. |
| `quality.pyscn` | pyscn analysis without opening the report, then `pyscn-check`; reports both failures. What `quality` runs. `--write` updates the badge. |
| `quality.pyscn-analyze` | Full pyscn analysis with HTML report, opened unless in CI. Reports whether the pyscn badge still matches the grade it measured; `--write` updates it. |
| `quality.pyscn-check` | CI-friendly pass/fail quality gate. Writes no report, so it has no grade to offer the badge — `quality.pyscn-analyze` is what produces one. |

## `build` — distributions

| Task | Args | What it does |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `build` | — | `secure.sbom`, then `build.package`, reporting both failures. What `preflight` and `release.dist` run. |
| `build.package` | — | `uv build` into `dist/`, then `twine check --strict` and `check-wheel-contents` over the wheel and sdist. Run alone, it packages whatever SBOM is already in `src/{{ project_slug }}/`. See [Packaging conformance](../explanation/packaging-conformance.md). |

## `release` — versioning and publishing

| Task | Args | What it does |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `release` | `-i <type>`, `--no-push` | Full release flow: validate, branch, bump, changelog, push, open PR/MR. |
| `release.validate` | — | Working-tree-clean and in-sync-with-origin checks. |
| `release.bump` | `-i <type>` | Refresh the README version badge, then `cz bump` with the given increment (major/minor/patch/alpha/beta/rc). The badge goes first so `cz bump` sweeps it into the commit the release tag points at — otherwise a release lands a README contradicting the version it released. |
| `release.changelog` | `--write` | Generate changelog. `--write` persists and commits, signing per `commit.gpgsign`. |
| `release.push` | — | `git push` + `git push --tags`. |
| `release.dist` | — | Audit dependencies, then clean and build into `dist/` (with `build.package`'s checks), leaving the artifacts for inspection or attestation. The audit is here rather than in `build`: publishing is where a live advisory should stop you. |
| `release.attest` | — | Sign a PEP 740 attestation for each wheel and sdist in `dist/`, written beside it as `<file>.publish.attestation`. Uses the CI job's OIDC token; locally it opens a browser to sign in to Sigstore. Does nothing when `UV_PUBLISH_*` credentials are set, since that path uploads no attestations. Fails if `dist/` holds no distribution. See [Verify a release attestation](../how-to/verify-a-release-attestation.md). |
| `release.publish` | `--prebuilt` | Build + `uv publish`. With `UV_PUBLISH_*` set it uploads with those credentials and without attestations; otherwise it requires an OIDC token and publishes through Trusted Publishing, attestations included. Invoked from CI on release-tag merge. `--prebuilt` publishes what `release.dist` already built instead of rebuilding. See [Set up Trusted Publishing](../how-to/set-up-trusted-publishing.md). |
| `release.clean` | — | Remove `dist/` and the SBOM written to `src/{{ project_slug }}/sbom.cdx.json`. |

## `secure` — pip-audit, SBOM

| Task | Args | What it does |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `secure.audit` | — | pip-audit honoring `.security-overrides`. Runs on a schedule, on pushes that change the dependency surface, and before publishing — deliberately not on every push, and not from `build`. |
| `secure.sbom` | — | The half `build` depends on: `sbom-extract --write` + `sbom-validate`. |
| `secure.sbom-extract` | `--write` | Compose a CycloneDX 1.7 SBOM with metadata header (lifecycles, tools, supplier, authors), a two-level dependency graph (project → runtime + dev + build-environment; build-environment → vendored + pipeline), and per-component licence + hash + external-reference enrichment. Prints to stdout; with `--write` lands at `src/<slug>/sbom.cdx.json` so `uv build` ships it inside the wheel. |
| `secure.validate-overrides` | — | Check every `.security-overrides` entry (format, real expiry date, no merge-conflict markers) without running the audit. The commit hook runs it when the file is staged. |
| `secure.sbom-validate` | — | Validate the SBOM against the CycloneDX 1.7 JSON schema (runs the validator in a clean `uv run python` subprocess so the venv-installed `jsonschema` wins over the older vendored one). Re-runs sbom-extract first if the file is missing. |

## `document` — documentation site

| Task | Args | What it does |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `document.build` | — | `properdocs build` — generate the static site under `site/`. |
| `document.serve` | — | `properdocs serve` — serve the site locally with live reload (Ctrl-C to stop). |
| `document.view` | — | Open the rendered site in the default browser (skipped in CI). |
| `document` (aggregator) | — | `build` + `view`. Writes no badge: building docs is not changing your README, and every other task here stopped writing unasked. `preflight --write` refreshes all five of them. |
{%- if integrate_pages and git_hosting_service == 'github' %}
| `document.deploy-github` | — | `properdocs gh-deploy --force` — build and publish to the `gh-pages` branch (consumed by the shipped Pages workflow). Only present when `integrate_pages=true` and `git_hosting_service=github`. |
{%- endif %}

## `container` — OCI deps image

| Task | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| `container.build` | Build `{{ project_slug }}-deps:latest` locally. |
| `container.publish` | In CI: push to the chosen host's registry. Locally: build only. |

## Shared helpers (not tasks)

Functions in `_CI/tasks/shared.py` (notably the `@logged` decorator, the `IndentingStream` plumbing, `execute`, `run_steps`) are used *by* tasks and not directly callable. See [The _CI tasks architecture](../explanation/the-ci-tasks-architecture.md).

Host-specific helpers live in `_CI/tasks/{{ git_hosting_service }}.py` and are imported by `container.py` and `release.py` via a Jinja-substituted import.
